<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charford</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cerdicesford</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charford is a place on the River Avon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>, south of Salisbury, usually identified with Cerdicesford, meaning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdic">Cerdic</a>'s ford. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> records that Cerdic and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cynric">Cynric</a> fought the Britons there in 519 and took the kingdom, an event treated as the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>, though historians regard the Chronicle's dates for these events as artificial.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charford.jpg" alt="Charford" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roy Hughes · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lyttleton_Avenue,_Charford.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
